WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.347
Matrices move space.

00:00:06.947 --> 00:00:09.453
Last time every crate got a place on my chart.

00:00:09.773 --> 00:00:12.173
Tonight the crane moves all of them at once.

00:00:13.373 --> 00:00:15.836
A crane that lifts one crate is useful.

00:00:16.156 --> 00:00:22.429
This crane takes the whole chart, and every crate on it, and turns it, or stretches it, or leans it over, in one go.

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:26.040
A matrix is that kind of move written as a grid of numbers.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:33.160
It sends every place on the chart somewhere, it keeps straight lines straight, and it leaves the corner where it is.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.791
Here's how to read one.

00:00:36.111 --> 00:00:41.185
Forget the crates and watch two short arrows from the corner: one step east, one step north.

00:00:41.785 --> 00:00:45.827
The first column of the matrix says where the east step lands.

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:49.410
The second column says where the north step lands.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:52.052
That is all a matrix has to say.

00:00:52.372 --> 00:01:00.983
Every place on the chart is so many east steps plus so many north steps, so it lands at the same number of the new east steps plus the new north steps.

00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:05.530
A quarter turn sends east to north, and north to west.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.861
Columns: zero, one, and minus one, zero.

00:01:09.461 --> 00:01:13.019
A stretch sends east to two steps east and leaves north alone.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:18.623
Every patch of the dock gets twice the area, and that factor has a name: the determinant.

00:01:19.223 --> 00:01:23.096
A lean, called a shear, slides the top of the chart sideways.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:28.995
Squares become slanted boxes, and yet the area stays the same: determinant one.

00:01:30.195 --> 00:01:31.901
Two cranes, one after the other.

00:01:32.221 --> 00:01:35.315
The first stretches the dock east, twice as wide.

00:01:35.635 --> 00:01:37.804
The second turns it a quarter turn.

00:01:38.404 --> 00:01:40.319
Does it matter which crane goes first?

00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:48.398
Stretch then turn sends the east step to 0, 2: two steps north.

00:01:48.718 --> 00:01:52.866
Turn then stretch sends it to 0, 1: one step north.

00:01:53.186 --> 00:01:54.849
So yes, the order matters.

00:01:55.449 --> 00:02:00.438
Two moves in a row are still one move, and its matrix is the product of the two.

00:02:00.758 --> 00:02:07.558
Multiplying matrices means doing the moves one after the other, and that is why the order of a product matters.

00:02:08.758 --> 00:02:10.000
Here's a harder one.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:14.179
Can any matrix move the corner of the chart?

00:02:16.679 --> 00:02:23.395
No. A matrix times a list of zeros gives a list of zeros, so the corner always stays put.

00:02:23.715 --> 00:02:27.147
To slide the whole chart over, you add a fixed list after the move.

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:33.957
Many language models do exactly that: a matrix, then a list added on, called a bias.

00:02:34.277 --> 00:02:37.435
Every layer of the model from the attention lesson does it.

00:02:38.635 --> 00:02:44.361
Back at the lighthouse, in the attention lesson, the word 'the' asked a question and kettle answered.

00:02:44.681 --> 00:02:46.892
Tallow skipped how the question was made.

00:02:47.212 --> 00:02:48.391
A matrix made it.

00:02:48.991 --> 00:02:54.717
By the second layer, the list of 32 numbers at each word has been through a step called layer normalisation.

00:02:55.037 --> 00:03:02.068
It shifts the numbers to average zero and scales them to a standard spread, then applies a learned scale and shift of its own.

00:03:02.388 --> 00:03:07.862
Then it meets the head's query matrix: 32 rows by 16 columns, plus a bias.

00:03:08.462 --> 00:03:12.083
Out comes the question, a list of 16 numbers.

00:03:12.403 --> 00:03:18.277
Kettle's list goes through a different matrix, the key matrix, and comes out as a label of the same length.

00:03:18.877 --> 00:03:25.087
Multiply the question and the label number by number and add it up: 41.28.

00:03:25.407 --> 00:03:29.176
Divide by 4, the square root of 16: 10.32.

00:03:29.496 --> 00:03:34.696
That is the score the attention film showed, rebuilt here from the trained weights.

00:03:35.896 --> 00:03:38.970
A matrix can only do moves that keep lines straight.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:43.647
Stack ten of them and you still have one straight-line move: their product.

00:03:44.247 --> 00:03:47.342
So a model puts bends between its matrices.

00:03:47.662 --> 00:03:57.241
In the one from the attention lesson, a smooth curve called GELU sits between the two matrices in the second half of each layer and bends every number that passes through.

00:03:57.561 --> 00:04:01.603
The softmax and the layer normalisation bend things too.

00:04:02.203 --> 00:04:07.740
How a model learns the numbers in its matrices is where the next lessons along the hill go.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.530
A matrix moves every place at once.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:18.081
Read its columns to see where the steps go, multiply two to do one move after another, and mind the order.

00:04:18.681 --> 00:04:21.902
Next along the hill, we find out which way is downhill.

00:04:22.222 --> 00:04:23.633
Goodnight.

